hey there educators my name is eddie wu  i'm head teacher mathematics at  cherrybrook technology high school in  new south wales and it's my privilege to  be hosting edu tweeddaws this week and  whether you're a pre-service early  career or very experienced teacher i  hope you'll be able to take part in the  discussions this week be exposed to new  and interesting ideas in education and  even make a few friends along the way if  you're new to twitter or edu twitters  like i was last year then you might find  this whole thing a bit confusing and not  really know where to start so let me try  and give you a brief introduction to  what this whole thing is and give you a  few tips on how to make the most of it  in my head edgy tweet artist is two main  things  firstly it's the twitter account at edu  tweedles which is where you probably saw  the link to this video every week an  australian educator from a different  state and different sector of education  will take over the account and use it to  lead discussions about various education  related issues that they're passionate  about to get an idea of who's done it in  the past you can head to edutweetoz.org  and read interviews of previous hosts  if you're in education and haven't  followed the attendee tweet oz account  yet i can't recommend it strongly enough  if you're following at the very least  you'll be able to listen in on some  fantastic and relevant conversations  about education in our country it's also  more than likely that you'll be able to  connect with some amazing people whose  experiences and expertise you can  benefit from in the long run  one day as i have you might even get to  meet some of them in person which is  great fun apart from the edu tweet oz  account there's also the edu tweet oz  now if you're new to twitter then  hashtags may still be something of a  mystery to you  the easiest way to think about hashtags  is that they're a way to connect  individual tweets and links and pictures  to a broader topic if you go to twitter  and search for something like hashtag  edchat then you'll see a whole bunch of  people talking about issues and ideas in  education  edgy tweet ours as you might have  guessed is where the aussies hang out it  can sometimes be a little difficult to  keep track of everything so when i'm at  my desk i use tweetdeck this allows you  to customize things and see everything  that you want all in one screen and it  automatically streams in new content on  anything you tell it to search for  if you're interested in learning how to  set it up but that looks a bit  intimidating then let me know and i'll  be happy to explain to get the most out  of edu twitters and the discussions that  will be happening this week the main  thing is participate  follow the account and the hashtag and  reply whenever someone interests you  retweet things that you feel are worth  remembering or sharing  favorite things that you agree with or  you think are particularly insightful  and engage with the other educators who  you see posting ask lots of questions  follow people who are in fields that  relate to you and who post things you  like and make sure you follow people you  disagree with too because one of the  best things about twitter is that it  isn't an echo chamber if you keep your  ears open you'll read lots of things  that challenge your assumptions and  practices and that's a good thing lastly  have fun it's a fantastic community to  be a part of it's full of really  supportive people and some evenings it  really does feel like everyone's just  having a great old chat in the staff  room we'd love for you to join us
 

